{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"feisty-intense-mandrill","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\nThe config-root guard flags `os.path.dirname(tracker)` but **does NOT flag** `os.path.dirname(StorePaths(tracker).canonical)`. Both compose the repo/config root from the store path — the precise construct the guard exists to eliminate — but only the first is detected.\n\nMeasured on 2026-08-28 while adjudicating `scathing-custommade-bobcat` (6bf1-326b-7404-4034): both forms were placed in one file under `src/`; the guard flagged the bare form and passed the `.canonical` form.\n\n## Why this matters more than a missed lint\n\nThe guard is what makes an acceptance criterion like 6bf1 AC#3 (\"ZERO deferred config-root sites\") meaningful. A construct the guard cannot see would sit in the tree **unflagged**, while that AC truthfully reports zero — so the criterion would pass while the defect class it protects is present. A guard with a blind spot is worse than a missing guard, because it converts an unchecked assumption into a checked-looking one.\n\nThis is not hypothetical: a concurrently-proposed redesign of `run_sweep` used exactly `dirname(canonical tracker)` as its fallback arm and would have entered the tree unsanctioned.\n\n## Mechanism\n\nBy its own docstring the detector matches the bare name, `*.tracker_dir()`, and `str()`-wrapped forms, and deliberately does **not** unwrap the `realpath`/`abspath` family. `StorePaths(...).canonical` is in that unwrapped family. The guard remedy text already names `config.repo_root_or_none()` as the sanctioned fix, so the intended contract is clear; the detector simply does not cover this spelling.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The guard flags `os.path.dirname(StorePaths(tracker).canonical)` in a file under `src/`.\n- [ ] The guard flags the equivalent `realpath`/`abspath`-wrapped spellings of the same composition, or the decision to exclude each is recorded in the guard docstring with its reason.\n- [ ] No existing sanctioned site newly fails: the guard still exits 0 on the current tree, or every new finding is triaged and either fixed or sanctioned.\n- [ ] The detector change is proven non-vacuous: a seeded positive is flagged AND the clean tree passes, so the test cannot pass by never running.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Add a guard self-test that seeds each newly-covered spelling and asserts a finding, plus a negative control asserting the clean tree passes.\n- Mutation-check: disable the new matching arm and confirm the seeded case survives (proving the assertion is load-bearing). Verify the seed actually applied — anchor the edit to a line start, since a replace that lands in a comment produces a mutant that was never seeded.\n- Run the full guard over the repo and record the before/after finding counts.","id":"9058-0b67-4c6a-49c4","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Config-root guard misses dirname(StorePaths(tracker).canonical), the exact construct it exists to catch"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787958934809905001,"uuid":"0c28392e-45c0-4600-a674-9ec28cd6297a"}